;; data/sanctions-sources.edn
;;
;; The screening authorities this service screens against, and the regulation
;; behind the one numeric default that is hard-coded in the request path.
;;
;; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
;; --------------------
;; app/api/screen/transaction/route.ts falls back to a threshold of 10000 when
;; ksqlDB does not answer, and components/rules-engine/* let an operator move
;; name-match confidence and country risk. None of those numbers said whose rule
;; they implement. A sanctions screener whose lists have no named publisher is
;; not auditable: a reviewer cannot ask "which list, as published when, by whom".
;;
;; EVERY :source/url BELOW WAS FETCHED. The :source/http-status and
;; :source/observed values are measurements from the run recorded in
;; :catalog/measured-at -- not descriptions of what the endpoint ought to return.
;; scripts/verify-sanctions-sources.cljs re-fetches all of them and fails when a
;; citation stops resolving, so this file cannot rot quietly.
;;
;; :source/http-status is the status of a plain GET. The verifier issues a RANGE
;; request (these documents run to 28 MB) and normalises 206 to 200 -- a partial
;; response and a full response say the same thing about whether the citation is
;; alive. 403/404/5xx are not normalised.

{:schema "cloud-itonami.screener/sanctions-sources-v1"
 :catalog/measured-at "2026-08-21T02:34:46Z"
 :catalog/measured-from "curl, macOS, direct egress (no proxy)"

